00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:10.550
Phil: The individuals we support, very
often, routine is a big thing and the need

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:18.050
for a routine and a lack of routine would
result in dysregulation and be a trigger

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:23.730
for you know, coming off of baselines
and escalating into crisis  points.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:27.590
Having a, I mentioned earlier, using
the teach approach, having visual

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:32.835
schedules, very clear what's happening
at this point and what's coming

00:00:32.835 --> 00:00:35.894
next,  and who is supporting as well.

00:00:35.894 --> 00:00:38.834
So who's going to be working
with me at this point.

00:00:38.834 --> 00:00:40.974
So you can see that predictability.

00:00:42.305 --> 00:00:46.175
Zoe: Children that have struggled
with processing routines.

00:00:46.805 --> 00:00:49.775
They struggled with understanding
when they came into school,

00:00:49.775 --> 00:00:51.214
what they needed to do.

00:00:51.798 --> 00:00:56.795
And we really supported them mainly
through visual timetables, visual guides

00:00:56.855 --> 00:01:02.485
and schedules so that they had a way of
referring back to the visual schedule

00:01:02.684 --> 00:01:04.285
and working out what they needed to do.

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:11.835
Kerry: A young person I support really
struggles with a sense of time and that

00:01:11.835 --> 00:01:14.235
time moves on and that things run out.

00:01:14.985 --> 00:01:22.995
And when I first started supporting them,
they could spend all day eating breakfast.

00:01:24.445 --> 00:01:26.085
There was a visual schedule in place.

00:01:26.414 --> 00:01:28.275
The classroom around them was moving on.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:35.400
But because that there was, yeah, they
just didn't have that sense of time

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:41.670
moves forward and there's an urgency
to some of the things that we do, so we

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.580
did a lot of support at home and we've
done a lot of support in school and what

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:49.200
we found works for them in particular,
in this occasion, especially around

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.890
eating, not so much for other tasks,
but we have a traffic light timer.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:57.630
So we used a visual traffic light timer
where they can't see the time countdown,

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.315
but it's green for about 70% of the time.

00:02:00.315 --> 00:02:03.285
And then it goes amber
for 85% of the time.

00:02:03.645 --> 00:02:06.884
And then it and will
flash red  at the end.

00:02:08.315 --> 00:02:11.734
Phil: I mean, I work with a couple
of young people at the minute and

00:02:11.734 --> 00:02:13.685
they have no real concept of time.

00:02:15.045 --> 00:02:16.214
They might be able to...

00:02:16.274 --> 00:02:18.185
they can tell you the time from the clock.

00:02:20.225 --> 00:02:24.454
But for instance, you could say
this will happen in five minutes.

00:02:25.304 --> 00:02:31.065
They can tell you, oh, that will be
a 4:29, 5 minutes from now, but the

00:02:31.065 --> 00:02:34.785
concept of, you know, even what a
minute is, is difficult to understand.

00:02:34.785 --> 00:02:39.524
So having that structure, that routine,
the visual schedule that helps at

00:02:39.524 --> 00:02:45.554
least sort of cope with, or give some
understanding of, you know, time in the

00:02:45.554 --> 00:02:47.174
chronological sort of linear fashion.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.540
Zoe T: Probably fair to say that
most people with autism would have

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.700
difficulty processing information
and would need more processing time.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:57.750
And so...

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:02.910
what you do with that is just give them
more processing time and potentially

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.010
sort of prompts them if you can.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.250
Yeah, the question about
routines, I think is more about

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.070
unpredictability and uncertainty.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:15.960
And my answer to that one would be
that you try to support the person

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.880
with autism to master the developmental
milestones that they need to master

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.800
in order to manage uncertainty and
unpredictability for themselves.

